-- title:
--   Pairing annihilator of identity-reducing corner points is inertia-cyclotomic
-- statement:
--   The statement is a single equivalence, formulated in a fixed ambient situation whose data are listed as hypotheses. Throughout, $J_H(M)$ denotes [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127), the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the geometric function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, and $T_pJ_H(M)$ its Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J_H(M)$ with $p^nx_n=0$ and $px_{n+1}=x_n$.
--
--   **(1) Level data.** A prime $p$ with $p\neq 2$; a natural number $M\neq 0$ with $p\mid M$ and $p^2\nmid M$; a subgroup $H\le(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ such that every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial lies in $H$ (`hHp`); a set $S$ of natural numbers; and `hin`, the hypothesis `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`, which requires the Hecke inputs `HeckeInputsHAlong` over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ at every prime $\ell$ together with, for each $d\in(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, an automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` realising the diamond operator $\langle d\rangle$.
--
--   **(2) Hecke algebra and corner idempotent.** A commutative ring $\mathbb{T}$ which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and acts on $T_pJ_H(M)$ compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action, faithfully (`hfaith`: an element annihilating all of $T_pJ_H(M)$ is zero); a map $\mathrm{op}$ from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q\mid M$, and $\langle d\rangle$ for $d\in(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$) to $\mathbb{T}$ with `hop`: $\mathrm{op}(g)$ acts on $T_pJ_H(M)$ as `tateGenOpH`, the endomorphism of the Tate module induced by the operator `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H(M)$; and `hgen`: $\mathbb{T}$ is generated over $\mathbb{Z}_p$ by the image of $\mathrm{op}$. Further, $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb{T}$, i.e. a finite family of complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals of $\mathbb{T}$ and satisfying $e_i\in\mathfrak{m}_j\iff i\neq j$; an index $i_0$ is given, with `hord`: $\mathrm{op}(U_p)\notin\mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$.
--
--   **(3) Place, model curve and Néron object.** A valuation subring $Pl$ of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ (that is, $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$), whose residue field has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed; `hj`: the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the level-one $q$-expansion field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`; a model $\mathfrak{X}$ of type `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` (in particular a curve model `𝔛.Meta` of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ with its comparison isomorphism `𝔛.eeta` to the geometric fibre of the integral model, and the section at infinity `𝔛.εinf`); level data $\Lambda$ of type `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`; `hΛ`, asserting that $\Lambda.f$ is smooth and proper with connected fibres and admits a relative group law; and an object $O$ of type `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, supplying a scheme $O.G$ over the base `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M)$ to the generic-fibre points of $O.g$, Hecke endomorphisms, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose associated point extends to the place, `ExtendsToPlace`) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ (generated by the toric points), as well as the integer $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$.
--
--   **(4) The Abel–Jacobi pin.** A block of hypotheses, summarised here, pinning down $O$ as the relative Picard scheme together with an explicit Abel–Jacobi map. It consists of: `hD` and `hDQ`, stating that the designation $(O.G,\,O.g,\,\text{identity section of }O.L)$ represents, with respect to the section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ and the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (rigidified line bundles that are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero), the relative sub-Picard functor of the integral model curve `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj`, and likewise after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$; `hsep`, separatedness of the base change to $\mathbb{Q}$; a morphism $\mathrm{ajQ}$ from the $\mathbb{Q}$-curve to the base-changed Picard object over its base, a comparison morphism $kQ$ between the geometric and the $\mathbb{Q}$-fibre of the model, a morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}:\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C\to O.G$ and a geometric base point $\bar\varepsilon$; `hpoinc`, identifying the Poincaré bundle over $\mathbb{Q}$ with the base change of the one over the integral base; `hajQε`, that $\mathrm{ajQ}$ carries the section at infinity to the zero section; `hajQ`, that for every field $K$, every $K$-point $t$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ and every point $x$ of the base-changed curve the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajQ}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the base point, i.e. $\mathrm{ajQ}$ is the map $x\mapsto[x-\varepsilon]$; `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, the compatibilities expressing $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ as the composite of `𝔛.eeta`, $kQ$, $\mathrm{ajQ}$ and the first projection, its compatibility with the structure maps, and the normalisation of $\bar\varepsilon$; `hpts_law`, that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law obtained from `hD`; and `hAJ`, that for all geometric points $x,s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ equal to the base point there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ on the geometric function field with $D_v=(x)-(s)$ under the place parametrisation of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}$, such that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of its class in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$. Finally `hrepΛ` asserts the analogous representability statement for $(\Lambda.X,\Lambda.f,\text{identity section})$ over the level-$\Gamma_N$ model curve with the composed section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}\circ\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   **(5) The inertia ring.** A domain $R$ which is a Henselian local ring with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with a faithful $R$-algebra structure on $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, such that: `hRA`, the image of $R$ lies in $Pl$; `hRdvr`, $R$ is a discrete valuation ring; `hRirr`, $p$ is irreducible in $R$; `hRfix`, an automorphism $\sigma$ of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the inertia subgroup `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` if and only if it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise; and `hRmax`, every element of $Pl$ fixed by the whole inertia subgroup comes from $R$.
--
--   **(6) The finite-part $p$-divisible layer.** A $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R$ of height $h$ (a system of finite free cocommutative Hopf $R$-algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals); an additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M)$; and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ from $T_p(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}))$ to $T_pJ_H(M)$. The hypotheses are: `hΔinj`, injectivity of $\Delta$; `hΔlev`, that $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ is exactly the set of $\Delta$-images of level-$v$ points; `hΔgal`, equivariance of $\Delta$ for an automorphism of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and an $R$-automorphism agreeing with it; `hΔhecke`, that each Hecke generator $g$ is induced by a transition-compatible system of coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels, in the sense that $\Delta$ intertwines precomposition by $\varphi_v$ with `genOpH M H S g`; `he`, that $e$ is $\Delta$ applied coordinatewise; `heinj`, injectivity of $e$; `herange`, that a Tate vector lies in the range of $e$ precisely when its $n$-th coordinate lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$ for all $n$; `hegal`, Galois equivariance of $e$ for `JH.tateGaloisRep`; `hsat`, saturation of the range of $e$ ($py$ in the range implies $y$ in the range); `hcoker`, that the quotient of $T_pJ_H(M)$ by the range of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linearly isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$; and `htor`, that the points of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ are $\Delta$-images of level-$v$ points.
--
--   **(7) The Raynaud quotient.** A $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R$ of height $h_B$, a transition-compatible system of coalgebra maps $\psi_v:\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v\to\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ (`hψt`), a natural number $h'$, and the numerical hypotheses `hhB`: $h=O.\mathrm{toricRank}+h_B$, and `hhB2`: $h_B=2h'$. Further: `hψker`, that the $\psi_v$-image of a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ is trivial precisely when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; `hψsurj`, surjectivity of $\psi_v$ on $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-points; `hψred`, that if the $\psi_v$-image of a point $x$ reduces to the identity at $Pl$ (every coordinate $x(a)-\text{counit}(a)$ has $Pl$-valuation $<1$) then so does $x$; and `hperiod`, that for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the geometric function field and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y)=\sigma\cdot z-z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity at $Pl$.
--
--   **(8) The scheme-level block.** A ring homomorphism $\rho_h$ from the integral base ring `XHDRLevel.R p` to $R$ and morphisms $\iota_v:\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)\to O.G$, subject to: `hρh`, compatibility of $\rho_h$ with the structure maps into $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase`, that $\iota_v$ lies over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\rho_h$; `hιcl`, that the induced morphism to the fibre product is a closed immersion; `hιp`, that $\iota_v$ is killed by multiplication by $p^v$ in the relative group law; `hιpts`, that $\iota_v$ realises $O.\mathrm{pts}\circ\Delta$ on level-$v$ points; `hιmul`, that $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the relative group law on points with values in any $R$-algebra; `hιt`, compatibility with the transition maps; `hιhecke`, that for each Hecke generator there is a transition-compatible system $\varphi_v$ of coalgebra endomorphisms which both matches $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ through $\iota_v$ and induces `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$; and `hιfin`, that the morphism $j_v$ induced by $\iota_v$ into the fibre product of the $p^v$-kernel with $\mathrm{Spec}\,R$ is both an open and a closed immersion whose image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$.
--
--   **(9) The corner pairing.** Write $X$ for the image in $J_H(M)$, under the first-coordinate projection [`TateModule.proj p _ 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122), of the corner submodule $e_{i_0}\cdot T_pJ_H(M)$, and $Y$ for the image under the same projection of the range of a given $\mathbb{Z}_p$-endomorphism $e'$ of $T_pJ_H(M)$. Given also a function $B:J_H(M)\times J_H(M)\to\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, the hypothesis `hB` is the conjunction of: $e'\circ e'=e'$; $e'$ commutes with `JH.tateGaloisRep` for every automorphism of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; $B(x,y)^p=1$ for $x\in X$, $y\in Y$; additivity of $B$ in the first variable on $X$ and in the second variable on $Y$; nondegeneracy on each side (an $x\in X$ with $B(x,y)=1$ for all $y\in Y$ is zero, and an $y\in Y$ with $B(x,y)=1$ for all $x\in X$ is zero); inertia equivariance, $B(\sigma\cdot x,\sigma\cdot y)=\sigma(B(x,y))$ for $\sigma$ in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, $x\in X$, $y\in Y$; the two duality clauses that for $a\in X\cap O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$ one has $B(a,y)=1$ for all $y\in Y\cap O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$ if and only if $a\in X\cap O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p)$, and symmetrically for $y\in Y\cap O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$; the equality of cardinalities $\#(Y\cap O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p))=\#(X\cap O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p))$; and the equality of cardinalities of the inertia-cyclotomic parts of $Y$ and of $X$, where an element $z$ is called inertia-cyclotomic when $\sigma\cdot z=c\,z$ for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup and every natural number $c$ such that $\sigma\zeta=\zeta^c$ for all $\zeta\in\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta^p=1$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $y\in J_H(M)$ lying in $Y$ and in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$, the following two conditions are equivalent.
--
--   First: $B(a,y)=1$ for every $a\in J_H(M)$ which lies in $X$ and is of the form $a=\Delta(w)$ for some level-one point $w$ of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ (taken through `pointsMkAdd` at level $1$) such that $w$ reduces to the identity at $Pl$, i.e. $Pl$-valuation of $w(b)-\text{counit}(b)$ is $<1$ for every $b\in\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,1$.
--
--   Second: $y$ is inertia-cyclotomic, that is, for every $\sigma\in Pl.\mathrm{inertiaSubgroupIn}\,\mathbb{Q}$ and every natural number $c$ such that $\sigma\zeta=\zeta^{c}$ for all $\zeta\in\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta^{p}=1$, one has $\sigma\cdot y=c\,y$.
--
--   At the multiplicative-reduction prime $p$ of the level, this identifies, inside the adjoint corner of $J_H(M)[p]$ cut out by $e'$, the annihilator under the corner pairing $B$ of those corner points that come from level-one points of the finite-part $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ reducing to the identity at the place: it is exactly the inertia-cyclotomic part. It is used by the cardinality identity [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.ncard_corner_finPts_mul_toricPts_eq_ncard_reducesToOne_mul_cyclotomic_of_abelJacobiPin_of_levelData_of_algEquiv`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.ncard_corner_finPts_mul_toricPts_eq_ncard_reducesToOne_mul_cyclotomic_of_abelJacobiPin_of_levelData_of_algEquiv), which feeds the local analysis at $p$ in the level-lowering step.
